<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhul-Nunid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1030s–<a href="/years/1085">1085</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Banu Dhi al-Nun, Dhunnunids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dhul-Nunid dynasty (Banu Dhi al-Nun) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> family that ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> from the 1030s to 1085. From their lordships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ucles">Uclés</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huete">Huete</a> they were invited to Toledo, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-al-zafir">Ismail al-Zafir</a> founded the dynasty. Under al-Mamun (1043–1075) Toledo became one of the strongest taifas and a centre of science, but his grandson al-Qadir surrendered the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI of Castile</a> in 1085.</p></div>
